# Hrmor bringup design

The HRMOR special purpose register is available
on POWER9 and later. It enables Hypervisor Real Mode Relocation, which
allows the OS, along with firmware, to be relocated in real mode. This
is especially useful for handling exceptions at a relocated address
that cannot be controlled with HAIL, such as machine check exceptions
(MCE), system reset, and hypervisor maintenance interrupts (HV_MAINT).

Kernel running state:

Normal case:
|<---kernel---->|<-fw->|<---------->|
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                               RAM                                   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

HRMOR based relocation:
                                |<---kernel---->|<-fw->|<---------->|
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|    RAM                        |                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
                                | HRMOR

The current ppc64 kernel always assumes it is identity mapped.
HRMOR based relocation breaks this assumption.

The HRMOR register behaves as follows:
- In real mode (i.e. MSR_IR=0 && MSR_DR=0) and with bit 63 of the
  effective address (EA) unset:
  - EA <- EA | SPR[HRMOR]

For example, accessing address 0xC0FFEE in real mode with an HRMOR
value of 0x1000000 will redirect the access to 0x1c0ffee. However,
accessing 0x8000000000C0FFEE will still access RAM address 0xC0FFEE,
because bit 63 of the EA is set. This is useful when working with MMIO
addresses (such as XSCOM), since we don't want those addresses to be
relocated by HRMOR.

The kernel is normally compiled with 0xC000000000000000 as KERNELBASE,
due to the properties of the TYPE-4 address family. Since the top
nibble 0xC also sets bit 63, we can't run the kernel's C code with
HRMOR relocation active. If we try to run kernel source in real mode
(head_64s.S or early_setup), we end up referencing lower memory, which
causes problems.

To avoid this, the following flow is used:
- Firmware boots up at its normal position.
- Kernel boots up at its normal position.
- Kernel configures the Radix MMU for the relocated space.
- Kernel copies *all* [1] of lower memory to the relocated space.
- Kernel performs an rfid to the relocated space.
- Kernel sets SPRN_HRMOR.

Several changes are also included along the OPAL_CALL path so that it
does not resolve virtual addresses while in real mode. Bringing up
secondary CPUs is also challenging: copying skiboot to a new location
leaves secondary CPUs stuck in their idle loop, which is addressed
with a hook mechanism in skiboot.

This series currently keeps the logical steps separate from the setup
for reviewability; many of these patches should eventually be squashed.

Only bring-up and basic buildroot programs have been tested so far.
The following are not yet working properly:
1. CPU idle - currently no cpuidle path runs C source in real mode;
   this needs to be fixed.
2. Multiple CPU and NVMe, Because of some issues with xive Multiple
   CPUs cant be enabled with NVMe disk.
